    "done_when": "Context: item (3) of followup f-fsri-krylov-slice-2-review-findings. _qualified_krylov_step (nova/equilibrium/fixed_point.py:2693) is built on jax.custom_batching.custom_vmap (:2653), which has no reverse-mode rule, so a caller that reverse-differentiates through it either fails or silently differentiates something else. Its call sites today are fixed_point.py:3297, 3339, 3925, 5301 and 5395. The merged implicit picard derivative (f8cd09f83) is the positive case: its adjoint is a custom_linear_solve on the terminal map and never reverse-differentiates the Krylov step. Deliverable: tests/test_krylov_reverse_mode_guard.py. (1) jax.grad of the picard route on the small fixture that tests/test_picard_implicit_derivative.py uses returns finite gradients, the positive case. (2) Every function that calls _qualified_krylov_step is enumerated by inspecting fixed_point.py's source with ast. For each, assert that it sits under a custom_vjp, custom_jvp or custom_linear_solve rule, or that it is a forward-only route, named in an explicit allowlist with a one-line reason, so a new reverse-mode caller fails the test until it is classified. (3) A direct jax.grad through _qualified_krylov_step raises, documenting the missing rule. Gate, logged under the run directory: the file passes with JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu in place on this fleet allocation. The negative control, applied in a scratch copy that is not committed, adds a new function to fixed_point.py that calls _qualified_krylov_step with no derivative rule, and the census test must fail naming it. tests/test_picard_implicit_derivative.py adds no failure. Every uv command carries --no-sync and nothing runs uv sync. The manifest's status is one of complete, blocked or failed.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Add a test that fails when a reverse-mode caller reaches the Krylov step without a derivative rule",
